---
import CodeTabs from "../components/CodeTabs.astro";
import Picker from "../components/Picker.astro";
import Page from "../layouts/Page.astro";
import { url } from "../lib/url";
---

<Page
  title="oklch-picker · an OKLCH colour picker for every framework"
  description="An OKLCH colour picker for React, Vue, Svelte, Solid, and no framework at all. Every axis is a slider over a gamut cross-section. Zero runtime dependencies."
>
  <h1>An OKLCH colour picker that takes the gamut seriously</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    For React, Preact, Vue, Svelte, Solid, and for no framework at all. Zero
    runtime dependencies. The component is ~5.3&nbsp;kB gzipped, and the colour
    maths alone is ~1.8&nbsp;kB.
  </p>

  <Picker
    presets={[
      "oklch(0.72 0.19 35)",
      "oklch(0.8 0.17 85)",
      "oklch(0.75 0.16 145)",
      "oklch(0.7 0.15 255)",
      "oklch(0.65 0.22 305)",
    ]}
    caption="Live. Drag the plot, or any slider."
  />

  <p>
    Every axis is a slider over a <strong>gamut cross-section</strong>: the
    filled silhouette is the range that can actually be shown, so the reachable
    colours are visible instead of something you discover by dragging into a
    region that does nothing.
  </p>

  <h2>Why</h2>

  <p>
    Most pickers work in HSV and convert. If your design tokens are already
    OKLCH, that round-trip is lossy and the controls do not map onto what you
    store. This one works in OKLCH directly:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The chroma slider is bounded by what is reachable.</strong> The
      sRGB gamut is a lopsided solid in OKLCH, so peak chroma depends on both
      lightness and hue. A fixed <code>0..0.37</code> slider is up to
      <strong>87% dead travel</strong> at low lightness: the thumb moves and the
      colour does not change. Here the maximum is recomputed as the other axes
      move, so 95 to 100% of the track does something.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Out-of-gamut regions are hatched</strong>, on every axis.
      Lightness is unreachable at <em>both</em> ends at high chroma, and hue can
      be unreachable in the middle. A single boundary marker will not do.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Nothing out-of-gamut is ever emitted.</strong> Values are clamped
      by reducing chroma, keeping lightness and hue.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Push chroma up on the picker above and most hues cannot sustain it. The
    hatching and the notice both say so.
  </p>

  <h2>Install</h2>

  <CodeTabs
    name="home-install"
    snippets={[
      { label: "React", lang: "sh", code: "npm install @oklch-picker/react" },
      { label: "Vue", lang: "sh", code: "npm install @oklch-picker/vue" },
      { label: "Svelte", lang: "sh", code: "npm install @oklch-picker/svelte" },
      { label: "Solid", lang: "sh", code: "npm install @oklch-picker/solid" },
      { label: "No framework", lang: "sh", code: "npm install oklch-picker" },
    ]}
  />

  <CodeTabs
    name="home-usage"
    snippets={[
      {
        label: "React",
        lang: "tsx",
        code: `import { useState } from "react";
import { ColourPicker } from "@oklch-picker/react";
import "@oklch-picker/core/styles.css";

export function Example() {
  const [colour, setColour] = useState("oklch(0.7 0.15 255)");
  return <ColourPicker value={colour} onChange={setColour} />;
}`,
      },
      {
        label: "Vue",
        lang: "vue",
        code: `<script setup>
import { ref } from "vue";
import { ColourPicker } from "@oklch-picker/vue";
import "@oklch-picker/core/styles.css";

const colour = ref("oklch(0.7 0.15 255)");
<\/script>

<template>
  <ColourPicker v-model="colour" />
</template>`,
      },
      {
        label: "Svelte",
        lang: "svelte",
        code: `<script>
  import { ColourPicker } from "@oklch-picker/svelte";
  import "@oklch-picker/core/styles.css";

  let colour = $state("oklch(0.7 0.15 255)");
<\/script>

<ColourPicker bind:value={colour} />`,
      },
      {
        label: "Solid",
        lang: "tsx",
        code: `import { createSignal } from "solid-js";
import { ColourPicker } from "@oklch-picker/solid";
import "@oklch-picker/core/styles.css";

export function Example() {
  const [colour, setColour] = createSignal("oklch(0.7 0.15 255)");
  return <ColourPicker value={colour()} onChange={setColour} />;
}`,
      },
      {
        label: "No framework",
        lang: "html",
        code: `<link rel="stylesheet" href="https://esm.sh/@oklch-picker/core/styles.min.css" />

<oklch-picker value="oklch(0.7 0.15 255)"></oklch-picker>

<script type="module">
  import "https://esm.sh/oklch-picker/register";
<\/script>`,
      },
    ]}
  />

  <h2>Where next</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><a href={url("/docs/install")}>Install</a>, covering every package and the 0.2 migration</li>
    <li><a href={url("/docs/layouts")}>Layouts</a>, four arrangements, all live</li>
    <li><a href={url("/docs/gamuts")}>Wider gamuts</a>, P3 and Rec. 2020 as output spaces</li>
    <li><a href={url("/playground")}>Playground</a>, every prop with code you can copy</li>
    <li><a href={url("/api")}>API</a>, generated from the type declarations</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Browser support</h2>

  <p>
    Preset swatches need <code>oklch()</code> in CSS. That means Chrome/Edge
    111+, Safari 15.4+, Firefox 113+. The charts, tracks and previews are
    computed to hex, so they render anywhere.
  </p>
</Page>
